  "body": {
    "id": "thm:latent-dimension",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every \\((G,P)\\in\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{cert}}^{(q)}(K,J)\\), \\(\\operatorname{rank}(T_{12})=q^K\\), and consequently \\(K_{\\min}(P_X)=K\\) even when a competing realization with fewer \\(q\\)-state coordinates is not double triangular.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "G",
      "P",
      "q",
      "K",
      "J",
      "\\(\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{cert}}^{(q)}(K,J)\\)",
      "\\(T_{12}\\)",
      "\\(K_{\\min}\\)",
      "\\(P_X\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:positive-support",
      "ass:certificate",
      "def:certified-class",
      "thm:certificate-frontier",
      "lem:finite-latent-state-lower-bound"
    ],
    "justification": "The stronger joint-state lower bound makes the equality \\(K_{\\min}(P_X)=K\\) a proved consequence of the observed law.",
    "gap": "The binary rank exclusion does not supply the heterogeneous categorical certificate.",
    "consumer": "It selects the number of \\(q\\)-state skills before model fitting.",
    "proof_tex": "Lemma~\\textnormal{lem:finite-latent-state-lower-bound} gives \\(\\operatorname{rank}(T_{12})=q^K\\) and proves the stronger fact that every finite latent measurement representation has at least \\(q^K\\) positive-mass joint latent states.  In particular, consider a competing measurement model with \\(L\\) latent coordinates, each having \\(q\\) states.  Its joint latent vector has at most \\(q^L\\) positive-mass states.  The lemma therefore gives\n\\[\n q^K\\le |\\operatorname{supp}(H')|\\le q^L.\n\\]\nSince \\(q\\ge3>1\\), \\(L\\ge K\\).  The original certified realization itself has \\(K\\) such coordinates, so the defining minimum satisfies \\(K_{\\min}(P_X)\\le K\\); the preceding inequality gives the reverse bound.  Hence\n\\[\n K_{\\min}(P_X)=K.\n\\]\nThe target \\(K\\) is thereby proved equal to the observed-law functional \\(K_{\\min}(P_X)\\); the equality is not being imposed by definition.",
